\name{ComplexHeatmapLayerProcessor}
\alias{ComplexHeatmapLayerProcessor}
\title{ComplexHeatmap Heatmap Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads the \code{heat} layer of one ComplexHeatmap heat map from the
\code{HeatmapList} its drawing returned (\code{\link[=complexheatmap_capture]{complexheatmap_capture()}}): the one
heat map of a chart, or one of a list's.

\strong{Order.} The rows and columns are read in the order drawn, the heat
map's own row and column order in the drawn list: by the dendrograms
when clustered, by \code{row_order}/\code{column_order} when given, and in a list
by the order the list shares -- the main heat map's rows in a horizontal
list, its columns in a vertical one. Rows are top first, as maidr.js
reads \code{data.points}.

\strong{Splits.} A heat map split into slices (\code{row_split}, \code{row_km},
\code{column_split}, \code{column_km}) is read as one grid, its slices put side by
side in the order drawn, top to bottom and left to right. The gaps
between slices are not cells, and are not read: moving past the last
row of one slice reaches the first row of the next.

\strong{Values.} Each cell is the value of the heat map's own matrix (the
\code{matrix} slot), exactly; a missing or infinite value is a hole (\code{null}).
A value the colour mapping clips is still its own value, not the end of
the scale.

\strong{Labels.} The row and column labels drawn (\code{row_labels},
\code{column_labels}, which default to the matrix's dimnames); when the names
are not shown (\code{show_row_names = FALSE}), the matrix's dimnames; and for
an unnamed matrix, each row's or column's number in the matrix.

\strong{Selectors.} Each cell is selected by its own id, in a grid whose rows
run bottom first, as maidr.js's heat grid does. When the body was drawn
as a raster image there are no cells to select, and the layer is read
without highlighting; a message (class \code{maidr_complexheatmap_raster})
says so.
}
